{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["macOS"],"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"UnsafePointer","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Pointee","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":"> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"where","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Pointee","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":" : ~Copyable","kind":"text"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"mentions":["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift"],"kind":"mentions"},{"content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"You use instances of the "},{"code":"UnsafePointer","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" type to access data of a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"specific type in memory. The type of data that a pointer can access is the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pointer’s "},{"code":"Pointee","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" type. "},{"code":"UnsafePointer","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" provides no automated"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"memory management or alignment guarantees. You are responsible for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"handling the life cycle of any memory you work with through unsafe"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pointers to avoid leaks or undefined behavior."}]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Memory that you manually manage can be either ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"untyped"}]},{"text":" or ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"bound","type":"text"}]},{"text":" to a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"specific type. You use the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafePointer"},{"text":" type to access and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"manage memory that has been bound to a specific type.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding a Pointer’s Memory State","anchor":"Understanding-a-Pointers-Memory-State"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The memory referenced by an ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafePointer"},{"text":" instance can be in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"one of several states. Many pointer operations must only be applied to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pointers with memory in a specific state—you must keep track of the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"state of the memory you are working with and understand the changes to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"that state that different operations perform. Memory can be untyped and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"uninitialized, bound to a type and uninitialized, or bound to a type and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"initialized to a value. Finally, memory that was allocated previously may","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"have been deallocated, leaving existing pointers referencing unallocated","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"memory.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Uninitialized Memory","anchor":"Uninitialized-Memory"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Memory that has just been allocated through a typed pointer or has been","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"deinitialized is in an ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"uninitialized","type":"text"}]},{"text":" state. Uninitialized memory must be","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"initialized before it can be accessed for reading.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Initialized Memory","anchor":"Initialized-Memory"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"Initialized","type":"text"}]},{"text":" memory has a value that can be read using a pointer’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"pointee"},{"text":" property or through subscript notation. In the following","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"example, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ptr"},{"text":" is a pointer to memory initialized with a value of ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"23"},{"text":":","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let ptr: UnsafePointer<Int> = ...","\/\/ ptr.pointee == 23","\/\/ ptr[0] == 23"]},{"level":1,"type":"heading","text":"Accessing a Pointer’s Memory as a Different Type","anchor":"Accessing-a-Pointers-Memory-as-a-Different-Type"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"When you access memory through an ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafePointer"},{"text":" instance, the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Pointee"},{"text":" type must be consistent with the bound type of the memory. If","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"you do need to access memory that is bound to one type as a different","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"type, Swift’s pointer types provide type-safe ways to temporarily or","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"permanently change the bound type of the memory, or to load typed","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"instances directly from raw memory.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"An ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafePointer<UInt8>"},{"text":" instance allocated with eight bytes of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"memory, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"uint8Pointer"},{"text":", will be used for the examples below.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let uint8Pointer: UnsafePointer<UInt8> = fetchEightBytes()"]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"When you only need to temporarily access a pointer’s memory as a different","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"type, use the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"withMemoryRebound(to:capacity:)"},{"text":" method. For example, you","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"can use this method to call an API that expects a pointer to a different","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"type that is layout compatible with your pointer’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Pointee"},{"text":". The following","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"code temporarily rebinds the memory that ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"uint8Pointer"},{"text":" references from","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UInt8"},{"text":" to ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int8"},{"text":" to call the imported C ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"strlen"},{"text":" function.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["\/\/ Imported from C","func strlen(_ __s: UnsafePointer<Int8>!) -> UInt","","let length = uint8Pointer.withMemoryRebound(to: Int8.self, capacity: 8) {","    return strlen($0)","}","\/\/ length == 7"]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"When you need to permanently rebind memory to a different type, first","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"obtain a raw pointer to the memory and then call the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"bindMemory(to:capacity:)"},{"text":" method on the raw pointer. The following","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"example binds the memory referenced by ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"uint8Pointer"},{"text":" to one instance of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UInt64"},{"text":" type:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let uint64Pointer = UnsafeRawPointer(uint8Pointer)","                          .bindMemory(to: UInt64.self, capacity: 1)"]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"After rebinding the memory referenced by ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"uint8Pointer"},{"text":" to ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UInt64"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"accessing that pointer’s referenced memory as a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UInt8"},{"text":" instance is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"undefined.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["var fullInteger = uint64Pointer.pointee          \/\/ OK","var firstByte = uint8Pointer.pointee             \/\/ undefined"]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Alternatively, you can access the same memory as a different type without","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"rebinding through untyped memory access, so long as the bound type and the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"destination type are trivial types. Convert your pointer to an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafeRawPointer"},{"text":" instance and then use the raw pointer’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"load(fromByteOffset:as:)"},{"text":" method to read values.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let rawPointer = UnsafeRawPointer(uint64Pointer)","let fullInteger = rawPointer.load(as: UInt64.self)   \/\/ OK","let firstByte = rawPointer.load(as: UInt8.self)      \/\/ OK"]},{"level":1,"type":"heading","text":"Performing Typed Pointer Arithmetic","anchor":"Performing-Typed-Pointer-Arithmetic"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Pointer arithmetic with a typed pointer is counted in strides of the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pointer’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Pointee"},{"text":" type. When you add to or subtract from an ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafePointer"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"instance, the result is a new pointer of the same type, offset by that","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"number of instances of the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Pointee"},{"text":" type.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["\/\/ 'intPointer' points to memory initialized with [10, 20, 30, 40]","let intPointer: UnsafePointer<Int> = ...","","\/\/ Load the first value in memory","let x = intPointer.pointee","\/\/ x == 10","","\/\/ Load the third value in memory","let offsetPointer = intPointer + 2","let y = offsetPointer.pointee","\/\/ y == 30"]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"You can also use subscript notation to access the value in memory at a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"specific offset.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let z = intPointer[2]","\/\/ z == 30"]},{"level":1,"type":"heading","text":"Implicit Casting and Bridging","anchor":"Implicit-Casting-and-Bridging"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"When calling a function or method with an ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafePointer"},{"text":" parameter, you can pass","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"an instance of that specific pointer type, pass an instance of a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"compatible pointer type, or use Swift’s implicit bridging to pass a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"compatible pointer.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"For example, the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"printInt(atAddress:)"},{"text":" function in the following code","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"sample expects an ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafePointer<Int>"},{"text":" instance as its first parameter:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["func printInt(atAddress p: UnsafePointer<Int>) {","    print(p.pointee)","}"]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"As is typical in Swift, you can call the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"printInt(atAddress:)"},{"text":" function","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"with an ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafePointer"},{"text":" instance. This example passes ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"intPointer"},{"text":", a pointer to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"an ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"text":" value, to ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"print(address:)"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["printInt(atAddress: intPointer)","\/\/ Prints \"42\""]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Because a mutable typed pointer can be implicitly cast to an immutable","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pointer with the same ","type":"text"},{"code":"Pointee","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" type when passed as a parameter, you can","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"also call ","type":"text"},{"code":"printInt(atAddress:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with an ","type":"text"},{"code":"UnsafeMutablePointer","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" instance.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let mutableIntPointer = UnsafeMutablePointer(mutating: intPointer)","printInt(atAddress: mutableIntPointer)","\/\/ Prints \"42\""]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Alternatively, you can use Swift’s ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"implicit bridging"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" to pass a pointer to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"an instance or to the elements of an array. The following example passes a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pointer to the ","type":"text"},{"code":"value","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" variable by using inout syntax:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["var value: Int = 23","printInt(atAddress: &value)","\/\/ Prints \"23\""]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"An immutable pointer to the elements of an array is implicitly created when","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"you pass the array as an argument. This example uses implicit bridging to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pass a pointer to the elements of ","type":"text"},{"code":"numbers","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" when calling","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"printInt(atAddress:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let numbers = [5, 10, 15, 20]","printInt(atAddress: numbers)","\/\/ Prints \"5\""]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"You can also use inout syntax to pass a mutable pointer to the elements of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"an array. Because ","type":"text"},{"code":"printInt(atAddress:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" requires an immutable pointer,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"although this is syntactically valid, it isn’t necessary.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["var mutableNumbers = numbers","printInt(atAddress: &mutableNumbers)"]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"No matter which way you call ","type":"text"},{"code":"printInt(atAddress:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", Swift’s type safety","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"guarantees that you can only pass a pointer to the type required by the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"function—in this case, a pointer to an ","type":"text"},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"name":"Important","type":"aside","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The pointer created through implicit bridging of an instance"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"or of an array’s elements is only valid during the execution of the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"called function. Escaping the pointer to use after the execution of the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"function is undefined behavior. In particular, do not use implicit"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"bridging when calling an "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UnsafePointer"},{"type":"text","text":" initializer."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["var number = 5","let numberPointer = UnsafePointer<Int>(&number)","\/\/ Accessing 'numberPointer' is undefined behavior."],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"}],"style":"important"}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/swift\/unsafepointer"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"kind":"symbol","topicSections":[{"title":"Instance Properties","anchor":"Instance-Properties","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/UnsafePointer\/customPlaygroundQuickLook","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/UnsafePointer\/pointee"]},{"title":"Instance Methods","anchor":"Instance-Methods","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/UnsafePointer\/deallocate()","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/UnsafePointer\/pointer(to:)","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/UnsafePointer\/withMemoryRebound(to:capacity:_:)"]},{"title":"Subscripts","identifiers":["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/UnsafePointer\/subscript(_:)"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Subscripts"},{"title":"Type A